  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThis morning...Dry and mostly clear skies prevailed across the \nregion early this morning. However, patchy, shallow ground fog began \nto develop in north-central WI with some locations reporting \nvisibilities as low as 1/4 mile. Anticipate the lowest visibilities \noccurring in valleys and low areas. Any fog will lift soon after \nsunrise.\n\nToday through Friday...Non-impactful weather is expected during this \nforecast period as high pressure and mostly dry conditions are \nforecast. There are two possible exceptions to this: first, there is \na very low chance of showers (up to 20%) in far northeast WI late \nthis evening through tonight from a weak, moisture starved backdoor \ncold front, and two, a few showers (less than 15% chance) may \ndevelop off of lake and bay breezes Friday afternoon.\n\nThere is also a signal for fog development late tonight into Friday \nmorning with the high pressure overhead. However, fog development \nmay be limited depending on how much cloud cover there is from the \nbackdoor cold front. For now, it appears locations in central and \nnorth-central WI would be most susceptible to seeing at least patchy \nfog development.\n\nRest of the Forecast...Low forecast confidence is present for the \ndetails revolving around this weekend's precipitation chances due to \ntiming and placement differences. But, it appears the first chance \nof showers and storms looks to arrive late Friday night into \nSaturday morning, with periods of showers and storms continuing \nthrough early next week. Ensembles are in decent agreement with the \ninstability peaking on Sunday and Monday, leading to a greater \npotential for thunderstorms, perhaps strong/severe, during this \ntime. But, given the low forecast confidence in the finer details, \nwill continue to monitor. Regardless of the storm severity \npotential, this set up could bring periods of heavy rainfall to the \ndrought ridden forecast area.\n\nTemperatures...Through the start of the weekend, high temperatures \nwill hover around normal with values mainly in the 70s to low 80s. \nFor tonight, lows will range from the mid 40s to mid 50s. But \ndepending on how much cloud cover spreads over the area, a few \nlocations across the Northwoods could see lows drop in the upper \n30s. Late this weekend into early next week, temperatures will warm \nabove normal with highs in the low to mid 80s, and dew points \nincreasing into the 60s, and perhaps low 70s for some spots in east-\ncentral WI.",
